  "verified_against": {
    "source": "release-manifest.json",
    "agrees": true,
    "note": "Hashes were downloaded from the published v5.42.0 release assets and hashed independently, then checked again by re-fetching the URL this pin itself names. Only the pegasus artifact changed: install.sh and the two builder scripts are byte-identical to v5.28.0 and earlier. v5.42.0 reaches this distribution through the shared delegation criterion every agent here lazy-loads, and through the grant command this distribution ships under its own name. An agent read that criterion, judged two parts genuinely independent -- two repositories, neither needing the other's result -- and delegated them one after the other anyway, waiting for the first to finish before issuing the second. The file's gates settle whether to fan out and how to cut the work; nothing in it said that concurrency is a property of how the calls are issued, not of the parts. Independence decided in the gates buys nothing unless every independent call goes out in the same reply, before any result has come back: issue one, read what it returns, then issue the next, and sequential execution was chosen no matter what the gates concluded. The criterion now says so, and the guard pins that order rather than the word parallel, which was already in the file and proved nothing -- the first version of that guard covered only the surrounding description, and deleting the one sentence that actually instructs an agent left it green. Separately, granting a directory the deny floor covers used to report success and do nothing: the floor is written last, so the runtime's last-match resolution lands there and the grant never applies. It still succeeds and is still recorded, but the report now says it will never take effect and why. That is not the dormant case the manual already describes for an ordinary grant under an open baseline -- an ordinary one regains its meaning the day the baseline returns to ask, and this one never does. The check ports the runtime's own wildcard matcher instead of approximating it with a path-component test, and names the two behaviours of the original it does not reproduce along with the condition under which that stops being safe. This distribution changed nothing of its own for this pin beyond the alignment."
  }
